    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-332918",
      "problem": "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)-2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[1,\\text{ }-2]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[2,\\text{ }1]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below. The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}\\cdot {{field-1}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}\\cdot {{field-2}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{-3t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}}\nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14122747/assets/617f0b123b98086ed902041c1a601d44ac264ab97acfc9e2af25ca330699d98f.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-6}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line.\n\nSince ${e}^{2t}\\to $ \n{{field-7}}\nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-8}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$",
